>
> \(i\) Compliance with all applicable environmental laws and regulations, and no knowledge of any material environmental liabilities;
>
> \(j\) Compliance with ERISA and the Internal Revenue Code with respect to all employee benefit plans maintained by or contributed to by the Borrower or any Guarantor, including the representation that the Target\'s 342 employees do not give rise to any material underfunded pension or post-retirement benefit obligations; and
>
> \(k\) No default or event of default has occurred and is continuing under the credit facility or any other material agreement to which the Borrower or any Guarantor is a party.

**[Section 9: Affirmative Covenants (Summary)]{.underline}**

The Credit Agreement shall contain customary affirmative covenants, including but not limited to the following:

> \(a\) Delivery of audited annual consolidated financial statements of the Borrower and its subsidiaries within one hundred twenty (120) days following the end of each fiscal year, accompanied by an unqualified opinion of an independent registered public accounting firm acceptable to the Administrative Agent;
>
> \(b\) Delivery of unaudited quarterly consolidated financial statements of the Borrower and its subsidiaries within sixty (60) days following the end of each of the first three fiscal quarters of each fiscal year;
>
> \(c\) Delivery of an annual operating budget and business plan within sixty (60) days after the commencement of each fiscal year;
>
> \(d\) Delivery of a compliance certificate, signed by the chief financial officer of the Borrower, concurrently with each set of annual and quarterly financial statements, demonstrating compliance with all financial covenants and certifying the absence of any default or event of default;
>
> \(e\) Maintenance of insurance coverage on all assets and properties in types and amounts consistent with sound business practices and as reasonably satisfactory to the Administrative Agent;
>
> \(f\) Maintenance of all properties, equipment, and assets in good working order and condition (ordinary wear and tear excepted);
>
> \(g\) Compliance with all applicable laws, rules, and regulations, including all tax obligations, and timely filing of all required federal, state, and local tax returns;
>
> \(h\) Payment of all taxes, assessments, and governmental charges when due (including income, franchise, sales, and property taxes in all fourteen (14) states in which HIS has nexus), unless being contested in good faith by appropriate proceedings and for which adequate reserves have been established;
>
> \(i\) Prompt notification to the Administrative Agent of any material event, including any tax audit, proposed tax assessment, or tax-related proceeding involving the Borrower or any Guarantor in an amount exceeding \$500,000;
>
> \(j\) Maintenance of the corporate, limited liability company, or other organizational existence and good standing of the Borrower and each Guarantor in their respective jurisdictions of formation; and
>
> \(k\) Provision of reasonable access and inspection rights to the Administrative Agent and its representatives (including accountants and other advisors), upon reasonable notice, to the properties, books, and records of the Borrower and each Guarantor, during normal business hours.

**[Section 10: Negative Covenants (Summary)]{.underline}**

The Credit Agreement shall contain customary negative covenants, which shall restrict the Borrower and its subsidiaries from engaging in certain activities without the prior written consent of the Administrative Agent and the Required Lenders, subject to customary baskets, thresholds, and exceptions to be negotiated in the definitive documentation. Such negative covenants shall include limitations on:

> \(a\) **Additional Indebtedness:** Incurrence of additional funded or contingent indebtedness, subject to customary permitted indebtedness baskets, including purchase money indebtedness, capital lease obligations, intercompany indebtedness, and a general basket;
>
> \(b\) **Liens:** Creation or existence of liens on any assets of the Borrower or any Guarantor, other than permitted liens (including existing liens, purchase money liens, and customary exceptions);
>
> \(c\) **Fundamental Changes:** Mergers, consolidations, liquidations, dissolutions, or changes to the legal form of organization of any Loan Party;
>
> \(d\) **Asset Dispositions:** Sales, transfers, leases, or other dispositions of assets outside the ordinary course of business, subject to customary exceptions, de minimis thresholds, and the reinvestment rights described in Section 4.6;
>
> \(e\) **Restricted Payments:** Declarations or payments of dividends, distributions, redemptions, or repurchases of equity interests, subject to customary builder baskets (based on cumulative retained Excess Cash Flow), general baskets, and exceptions for tax distributions;
>
> \(f\) **Investments:** Making of loans, advances, or investments, subject to customary permitted investments baskets and exceptions for ordinary course investments;
>
> \(g\) **Transactions with Affiliates:** Entry into transactions with affiliates except on terms no less favorable than arm\'s length; management, monitoring, consulting, and advisory fees paid to the Sponsor or its affiliates shall be capped at the greater of \$2,000,000 per fiscal year or 2.0% of Adjusted EBITDA for the most recently completed trailing twelve-month period;
>
> \(h\) **Modifications to Organizational Documents or Material Contracts:** Amendments or modifications to organizational documents, the Acquisition Agreement, or other material contracts, in each case to the extent materially adverse to the interests of the lenders;
>
> \(i\) **Changes to Fiscal Year:** Changes to the fiscal year-end of the Borrower without the prior written consent of the Administrative Agent;
>
> \(j\) **Changes to Accounting Methods:** Changes to accounting methods or practices, including, specifically, no change to the LIFO inventory accounting method currently employed by HIS without the prior written consent of the Administrative Agent. The LIFO reserve of \$11,800,000 is a significant balance sheet item, and any change from LIFO to FIFO or other method could materially affect reported financial results and covenant compliance; and
>
> \(k\) **Prepayment of Junior Indebtedness:** Voluntary prepayment, redemption, or defeasance of any subordinated or junior indebtedness, if any, prior to its scheduled maturity.

**[Section 11: Events of Default]{.underline}**

The Credit Agreement shall contain customary events of default, including but not limited to the following:

> \(a\) Failure to pay any principal of the Term Loan when due and payable (whether at stated maturity, upon mandatory prepayment, acceleration, or otherwise);
>
> \(b\) Failure to pay any interest, fee, or other amount owing under the credit facility within five (5) business days of the date when due;
>
> \(c\) Breach of any financial covenant set forth in Section 7, subject to a cure period of fifteen (15) business days for the Fixed Charge Coverage Ratio covenant only (no cure period shall apply to any breach of the Total Net Leverage Ratio covenant);
>
> \(d\) Breach of any other covenant, representation, or warranty contained in the credit facility documentation, subject to a cure period of thirty (30) days (to the extent such breach is capable of cure) after written notice from the Administrative Agent;
>
> \(e\) Any representation or warranty made by the Borrower or any Guarantor proving to have been materially incorrect or misleading when made or deemed made;
>
> \(f\) Cross-default to any other indebtedness of the Borrower or any Guarantor in an aggregate principal amount exceeding \$5,000,000;
>
> \(g\) Commencement of any bankruptcy, insolvency, receivership, reorganization, or similar proceeding by or against the Borrower or any Guarantor;
>
> \(h\) Entry of one or more judgments against the Borrower or any Guarantor in an aggregate amount exceeding \$5,000,000, to the extent not covered by insurance or indemnification and not stayed, bonded, or discharged within sixty (60) days;
>
> \(i\) The occurrence of any ERISA event that could reasonably be expected to result in a material adverse effect;
>
> \(j\) A Change of Control, defined as: (i) Redstone Capital Partners Fund IV, LP (or any successor fund managed by Redstone Capital GP IV, LLC or its affiliates) ceasing to own, directly or indirectly, at least 50.1% of the outstanding equity interests (on a fully diluted basis) of the Borrower; or (ii) the Borrower ceasing to own, directly or indirectly, 100% of the outstanding equity interests of HIS; and
>
> \(k\) The occurrence of a Material Adverse Change (as defined in the Credit Agreement), meaning any event, circumstance, development, or condition that has had, or could reasonably be expected to have, a material adverse effect on (i) the business, assets, operations, financial condition, or results of operations of the Borrower and its subsidiaries taken as a whole, (ii) the ability of the Loan Parties to perform their obligations under the credit facility documentation, or (iii) the rights and remedies of the Administrative Agent and the lenders under the credit facility documentation.

Upon the occurrence of an Event of Default, the Administrative Agent may (and, at the direction of the Required Lenders, shall) declare all outstanding obligations under the credit facility to be immediately due and payable and exercise any and all remedies available under the credit facility documentation and applicable law, including enforcement of the security interests in the collateral.

**[Section 12: Conditions Precedent to Closing]{.underline}**

The obligation of the lenders to fund the Term Loan on the Closing Date shall be subject to the satisfaction (or waiver by the Administrative Agent and the Required Lenders) of the following conditions precedent, each in form and substance satisfactory to the Administrative Agent:

> \(a\) Execution and delivery by all parties thereto of the Acquisition Agreement (expected on or about December 6, 2024), and all conditions to the closing of the Acquisition thereunder having been satisfied or waived concurrently with the funding of the Term Loan;
>
> \(b\) Execution and delivery of the definitive Credit Agreement and all ancillary loan documents, including the security agreement, pledge agreement, guaranty agreement, mortgage documents, and related instruments;
>
> \(c\) Receipt of evidence satisfactory to the Administrative Agent that the Acquisition has been (or will be simultaneously) consummated in accordance with the terms of the Acquisition Agreement, with an enterprise value of not less than \$385,000,000 and on terms and conditions not materially different from those described in this Term Sheet;
>
> \(d\) Evidence that the Sponsor has contributed not less than \$185,000,000 in cash equity to the Borrower, representing not less than approximately 48.1% of the total sources of funds;
>
> \(e\) Evidence that the existing funded indebtedness of HIS (approximately \$47,300,000) will be repaid in full simultaneously with, or immediately prior to, the closing, and that all liens securing such indebtedness will be released;
>
> \(f\) Delivery of a solvency certificate executed by the chief financial officer of the Borrower, in form and substance satisfactory to the Administrative Agent, certifying the solvency of the Borrower on a consolidated basis after giving effect to the Acquisition, the incurrence of the Term Loan, and the payment of all fees, costs, and expenses;
>
> \(g\) Receipt of evidence of HSR Act clearance (or expiration or early termination of the applicable waiting period under the Hart-Scott-Rodino Antitrust Improvements Act of 1976, as amended);
>
> \(h\) Evidence of insurance satisfying the requirements of the Credit Agreement, with the Administrative Agent named as additional insured and loss payee, as applicable;
>
> \(i\) Completion of all filings and recordings necessary to perfect the Administrative Agent\'s first-priority security interests in the collateral, including UCC financing statements, intellectual property filings, and mortgage recordings;
>
> \(j\) No material adverse change in the business, assets, operations, financial condition, or prospects of HIS or the Borrower since September 30, 2024;
>
> \(k\) Delivery of customary legal opinions from Borrower\'s counsel, Ashworth, Bledsoe & Cahill LLP, addressing enforceability, corporate authority, no conflicts, and other customary matters;
>
> \(l\) Receipt of certificates of good standing for the Borrower from the Secretary of State of Delaware and for HIS from the Michigan Department of Licensing and Regulatory Affairs;
>
> \(m\) Receipt of a customary quality of earnings report (noting the report dated October 28, 2024, prepared by Ridgeline Accounting Group), with results satisfactory to the Administrative Agent; and
>
> \(n\) Payment of all fees and expenses described in Section 6 (Fees and Expenses), including the OID, Arrangement Fee, Placement Agent Fee, and legal fees and expenses of the Administrative Agent\'s counsel.

**[Section 13: Miscellaneous and Other Terms]{.underline}**

**Section 13.1: Governing Law**

The Credit Agreement and all ancillary loan documents shall be governed by, and construed in accordance with, the laws of the State of New York, without regard to conflicts of laws principles that would require the application of the laws of any other jurisdiction.

**Section 13.2: Assignments and Participations**

Each lender may assign or participate all or a portion of its commitments and loans under the credit facility, subject to customary restrictions, including: (a) a minimum assignment amount of \$1,000,000 (or such lesser amount as constitutes the assignor\'s entire remaining interest); (b) the prior written consent of the Administrative Agent (not to be unreasonably withheld, conditioned, or delayed); and (c) the prior written consent of the Borrower (not to be unreasonably withheld, conditioned, or delayed, and deemed given if the Borrower has not objected within ten (10) business days of notice), provided that the Borrower\'s consent shall not be required during the continuance of an Event of Default or for assignments to another lender or an affiliate of a lender, and provided further that no assignment may be made to a competing business or disqualified lender as identified on a list to be agreed upon by the Borrower and the Administrative Agent.

**Section 13.3: Confidentiality**

The Credit Agreement shall contain customary confidentiality provisions. All non-public information provided by the Borrower, the Sponsor, or their representatives to the Administrative Agent, the lenders, or their advisors shall be treated as confidential and shall not be disclosed to any third party, subject to customary exceptions for (a) disclosures required by law, regulation, or judicial or administrative process, (b) disclosures to regulatory authorities having jurisdiction over the lenders, (c) disclosures to prospective assignees or participants who have executed customary non-disclosure agreements, and (d) disclosures to the lenders\' professional advisors on a need-to-know basis.

**Section 13.4: Tax Provisions**

The Credit Agreement shall contain customary tax provisions, including the following:

> \(a\) **Gross-Up and Tax Indemnity:** If any withholding or deduction for taxes is required by law to be made from any payment under the credit facility, the Borrower shall pay such additional amounts as may be necessary to ensure that the net amount received by each lender (after giving effect to such withholding or deduction) equals the amount that would have been received had no such withholding or deduction been required.
>
> \(b\) **Tax Forms:** Each lender shall deliver to the Administrative Agent and the Borrower, on or prior to the date on which it becomes a party to the Credit Agreement (and from time to time thereafter as reasonably requested), properly completed and executed IRS Forms W-9 or, for non-U.S. lenders, the applicable IRS Form W-8 series (W-8BEN, W-8BEN-E, W-8ECI, or W-8IMY, as applicable).
>
> \(c\) **FATCA Compliance:** The Credit Agreement shall contain provisions addressing compliance with the Foreign Account Tax Compliance Act (\"FATCA\"), including Sections 1471 through 1474 of the Internal Revenue Code, as applicable.
>
> \(d\) **Withholding Tax Provisions for Non-U.S. Lenders:** Non-U.S. lenders shall be entitled to any reduced rate of, or exemption from, withholding tax to the extent provided by an applicable tax treaty or by law, provided that such lender has delivered all required documentation.

The Borrower acknowledges that the tax treatment of the Original Issue Discount (\$4,000,000), the Arrangement Fee (\$1,000,000), the Placement Agent Fee (\$500,000), and other financing costs, fees, and expenses described herein will be determined by the Borrower in consultation with its tax advisors, including with respect to (i) the amortization of such costs over the term of the Term Loan in accordance with Sections 163(e), 1272, and 1273 of the Internal Revenue Code and applicable Treasury Regulations (including Treasury Regulation Section 1.263(a)-5), (ii) the distinction between financing costs and acquisition costs for federal income tax purposes, and (iii) the deductibility of business interest expense, including the interest on the Term Loan and amortized OID, under Section 163(j) of the Internal Revenue Code.

**Section 13.5: Counsel**

**Administrative Agent\'s Counsel:** Ironclad National Bank\'s outside legal counsel (to be designated in the definitive Credit Agreement documentation).

**Borrower\'s Counsel:** Ashworth, Bledsoe & Cahill LLP, 200 S. Wacker Drive, Suite 3100, Chicago, IL 60606. Attention: Victoria Chen, Tax Partner, and Marcus Halpern, Engagement Partner.

The Borrower shall be responsible for the payment of the Administrative Agent\'s counsel\'s reasonable and documented fees and out-of-pocket expenses incurred in connection with the negotiation, preparation, and execution of the credit facility documentation, as more fully described in Section 6 (estimated at \$350,000 to \$500,000 through closing).

**Section 13.6: Syndication**

Ironclad National Bank, as Lead Arranger and Bookrunner, intends to syndicate the Term Loan to a group of institutional lenders following the Closing Date. The Borrower agrees to cooperate with Ironclad National Bank and Crestview Capital Markets in their reasonable syndication efforts, including without limitation (a) the provision of customary information packages and financial projections, (b) participation in lender meetings and presentations at reasonable times and locations, and (c) the use of reasonable best efforts to ensure that at least one credit rating is obtained from a nationally recognized statistical rating organization (Moody\'s Investors Service, S&P Global Ratings, or Fitch Ratings) in connection with the Term Loan. Crestview Capital Markets shall act as placement agent in connection with any such post-closing syndication.

**[Section 14: Indicative Timetable]{.underline}**

The following timetable is indicative only and is subject to change based on the progress of due diligence, documentation negotiations, and satisfaction of the conditions precedent described herein:

  ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  **Milestone**                                             **Target Date**
  --------------------------------------------------------- --------------------------------------
  Issuance of Commitment Letter by Ironclad National Bank   On or before November 29, 2024

  Execution of Definitive Acquisition Agreement             December 6, 2024 (estimated)

  Credit Agreement Documentation Period                     December 6, 2024 -- January 24, 2025

  Closing and Funding of Term Loan                          January 31, 2025 (estimated)

  Post-Closing Syndication                                  February -- March 2025
  ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The foregoing timetable assumes timely receipt of HSR Act clearance and no material delays in the documentation process. The parties shall use commercially reasonable efforts to adhere to the timetable set forth above.

